<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="salicylate biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-110 &quot;Salicylate&quot;| is an aromatic compound that is is common throughout the plant kingdom and is also found in some bacteria. Several bacterial genera utilize salicylate as a building block for low-molecular-weight organic Fe3+ chelators (siderophores), such as |FRAME: CPD-9991 yersiniabactin| in |FRAME: TAX-632 &lt;i&gt;Yersinia pestis&lt;/i&gt;| and |FRAME: TAX-630 &lt;i&gt;Yersinia enterocolitica&lt;/i&gt;|, |FRAME: CPD-9992 pyochelin| in |FRAME: TAX-287 &lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/i&gt;|, and |FRAME: CPD-12068 &quot;mycobactin&quot;| in |FRAME: TAX-1773 &lt;i&gt;Mycobacterium tuberculosis&lt;/i&gt;|. In bacteria |FRAME: CPD-110 salicylate| is derived from |FRAME: CHORISMATE chorismate| in two steps, via |FRAME: ISOCHORISMATE isochorismate|, as described in this pathway. Salicylate is also important in plants, where it induces flowering and is involved in resistance against pathogen infection through the systematic acquired resistance mechanism |CITS: [15283665][12873532]|. While plants can make salicylate in more than one pathway (see |FRAME: PWY-981 salicylate biosynthesis II|), genetic studies in Arabidopsis have shown that the bulk of salicylate is produced from chorismate |CITS: [11734859]|.</Description>
